  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{-2(1)^{4}+6(1)^{2}-4}{(1)^{2}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{-2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2({x}^{2}-2)({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =-2({x}^{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\,\\,-2({x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-2(1-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
